  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRRS SIDS OECS 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The coastal zones of the Eastern Caribbean are highly vulnerable to the impacts of climate change. Rising sea levels, accelerating coastal erosion, and increasingly intense storm surges threaten ecosystems, infrastructure, and livelihoods. In some areas of Grenada, for example, erosion rates have reached 1&#x2013;2 metres per year, while hurricane intensity across the region has increased by more than 10% over the past decade. Despite the urgency, fragmented data systems anduneven technical capacity across Member States of the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States (OECS) continue to impede timely, evidence-based decision-making for Integrated Coastal Zone Management (ICZM).In light of this, the OECS Commission has requested technical assistance through the Dutch Disaster Risk Reduction &amp; Surge Support (DRRS) Programme, specifically to support the development of a prototype for a Digital Earth Caribbean (DEC) - an intended regional geoplatform that enables advancedcoastline analysis and can quickly and data-driven respond to coastal risks.</narrative>
  </description>
